  const PHOTOS = [{"src": "/terroir/Kendwa-Unguja/img/kendwa-1.jpg", "caption": "Kendwa sand at the end of the day. The beach shelves steeply within metres, which is why the water is still here at low tide — the one stretch on Unguja where that is true.", "credit": "Zoheb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · Wikimedia Commons"}, {"src": "/terroir/Kendwa-Unguja/img/kendwa-2.jpg", "caption": "Nungwi. The north coast is Zanzibar's dhow-building centre: hulls go up on the open sand, shaped by hand with an adze and bent with fire, with no drawing anywhere.", "credit": "Moongateclimber · CC BY 3.0 · Wikimedia Commons"}, {"src": "/terroir/Kendwa-Unguja/img/kendwa-3.jpg", "caption": "A ngalawa — the double-outrigger canoe dug from a single mango trunk. The outrigger idea is Austronesian, and reached this coast with the movement that made Madagascar Malagasy-speaking.", "credit": "Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · Wikimedia Commons"}];
  const GEMS = [{"id": "gem-urojo", "name": "Urojo — the Zanzibar mix", "tag": "Food gem", "title": "A soup you assemble at the counter", "pattern": "urojo", "story": "Zanzibar mix: a sour, turmeric-yellow soup assembled at the counter like a kit. Where it really comes from, and how to judge one, is in La liste at the foot of this guide.", "body": "", "where": "Forodhani at dusk, and at Lukmaan's counter in Stone Town"}, {"id": "gem-zanzibar-pizza", "name": "Zanzibar pizza", "tag": "Food gem", "title": "Neither Italian nor traditional", "pattern": "Zanzibar pizza", "story": "Neither Italian nor in the Swahili canon: a griddled parcel born at the Forodhani stalls. The one rule for eating it is in La liste, at the foot.", "body": "", "where": "Forodhani Gardens night market, from about 18:00"}, {"id": "gem-kahawa", "name": "Kahawa chungu", "tag": "Food gem", "title": "Bitter coffee from a brass kettle", "pattern": "kahawa chungu", "story": "Kahawa chungu means bitter coffee, and the bitterness is the point: a strong black brew, spiced with cardamom, clove and sometimes ginger or cinnamon, kept hot in a tall brass kettle over charcoal and poured into small handleless cups. No sugar, no milk. It is sold from a stool at the edge of a market, drunk in three mouthfuls standing up, and the cup is handed back for the next person. On this coast it is the furniture of conversation — the drink men sit over at the edges of Darajani in the morning.", "body": "", "where": "The coffee men at the edges of Darajani market, Stone Town, from early morning"}, {"id": "gem-nazi", "name": "Mchuzi wa nazi", "tag": "Food gem", "title": "The mother sauce, and the split that proves it", "pattern": "mchuzi wa nazi", "story": "The coconut sauce every kitchen on this island starts from. How it is pressed and how to grade it at a glance are in La liste, at the foot.", "body": "", "where": "Machnoo and Kivulini in Nungwi village; Lukmaan in Stone Town"}, {"id": "gem-ngalawa", "name": "The ngalawa", "tag": "Craft gem", "title": "One mango tree, two outriggers", "pattern": "ngalawa", "story": "The double-outrigger dugout with the triangular sail you see on every stretch of this beach. How one is made is told in The boats &amp; the hand.", "body": "", "where": "Under construction on the open sand at the Nungwi dhow yards"}, {"id": "gem-karafuu", "name": "Karafuu — the clove", "tag": "Place gem", "title": "Three trees for every palm", "pattern": "karafuu", "story": "The clove: a flower bud, dried until it rattles. Why nine-tenths of the world's came from here is the opening of the food history; how to buy it is in La liste.", "body": "", "where": "Growing at Kizimbani; sold loose at Darajani market"}, {"id": "gem-mkate", "name": "Mkate wa kumimina", "tag": "Food gem", "title": "The bread you pour", "pattern": "mkate wa kumimina", "story": "'The bread that is poured': rice, coconut and time, with no wheat and no oven. A household bread, so you have to be lucky; its story is in La liste.", "body": "", "where": "At Lukmaan's counter in Stone Town when it is on, and in Nungwi village kitchens"}];
  const TABLES = {"grande": {"title": "Les Grandes Tables", "desc": "The rooms worth planning a day around — the domes, the design house, a Stone Town roof, the new wave, the beach houses, and the auctions that feed all of them.", "sections": [{"label": "La crème — the destination rooms", "desc": "Three rooms worth a taxi and a booking — the domes and the design house on Kendwa sand, and a Stone Town roof with taarab.", "ids": ["kilindi", "zuri-zanzibar", "emerson-hurumzi"]}, {"label": "The new wave", "desc": "The three kitchens on this cape trying something — ceviche on a west deck, a beach room that cooks, a laksa at seven in the morning.", "ids": ["sexy-fish", "maisha-beach", "yasa"]}, {"label": "The beach houses", "desc": "The two resort kitchens worth booking as a non-resident — choose by which way the room faces.", "ids": ["gold-zanzibar", "essque-zalu"]}, {"label": "Where the boats land — and the island grows", "desc": "The auctions, the market hall and the plantation: the three places on Unguja where food is actually priced.", "ids": ["mkokotoni", "darajani", "kizimbani"]}]}, "petite": {"title": "Les Petites Tables", "desc": "Village kitchens, Italian ovens and a Hanoi broth, the breakfast rooms, the fire, the late tables, the sand bars and the old houses — nearly all of it off the beach.", "sections": [{"label": "The Swahili kitchens", "desc": "Where the village eats — coconut, clove and rice at village prices, and every one of them off the sand.", "ids": ["combo-1990", "machnoo", "lukmaan"]}, {"label": "The other kitchens — Italian ovens and a Hanoi broth", "desc": "Italian-run kitchens and a Vietnamese broth: the arrivals that stayed and became part of the north tip.", "ids": ["mama-mia-nungwi", "bistro-del-mar", "hanoi-house"]}, {"label": "The breakfast tables", "desc": "The three rooms open before nine — Kilimanjaro coffee, a French bakery, and a table on the empty end of the beach.", "ids": ["jf-kili", "passion-thyme", "mahi-mahi"]}, {"label": "The fire", "desc": "Charcoal on the sand, a small hotel kitchen, and the weekly BBQ night.", "ids": ["baraka-beach-rest", "smiles-beach", "makofi"]}, {"label": "After service — where the strip eats late", "desc": "After the sunset rush: the kitchen that runs latest, lobster at a working wage, and the last room awake.", "ids": ["che-rock", "fisherman-local", "highland"]}, {"label": "The sand bars", "desc": "The sundowner belt — the quiet bar on Kendwa sand and the roof in Nungwi.", "ids": ["sunset-kendwa", "z-rooftop"]}, {"label": "The old houses", "desc": "The three addresses that were here before the strip was — and one that was built as a private house.", "ids": ["kendwa-rocks", "langi-langi", "cholos"]}]}, "street": {"title": "La Rue — The Street", "desc": "No tables. Charcoal on the sand, a griddle in a garden, a duka at dawn — and one rule: only what is cooked in front of you.", "sections": [{"label": "La Rue — the street", "desc": "Cooked in front of you, eaten standing up, paid for in shillings — and priced before it is made.", "ids": ["nungwi-fish-market", "forodhani", "nungwi-village"]}]}};
  const CATEGORIES = [{"key": "creme", "label": "La crème — the destination rooms", "lead": "Three rooms worth a taxi and a booking — the domes and the design house on Kendwa sand, and a Stone Town roof with taarab.", "story": {"title": "Taarab on the roof", "story": "Taarab is the sung poetry of the Swahili coast — strings, the qanun and percussion, shaped by a century of Egyptian and Indian records arriving by sea. In Stone Town it is still taught, and on the roof of Emerson on Hurumzi it is what you eat to, most evenings, sitting on the floor.", "where": "Emerson on Hurumzi, Stone Town"}}, {"key": "rising", "label": "The new wave", "lead": "The three kitchens on this cape trying something — ceviche on a west deck, a beach room that cooks, a laksa at seven in the morning.", "story": {"title": "What a monsoon crossroads collects", "story": "A laksa, a ceviche and a rösti on a beach four degrees south of the equator look like globalisation until you remember what this island has always been: for a thousand years the monsoon brought Oman, Gujarat and the Malabar coast here and took them home again. New cooking arriving and staying is the old pattern, not a new one.", "where": "Nungwi"}}, {"key": "houses", "label": "The beach houses", "lead": "The two resort kitchens worth booking as a non-resident — choose by which way the room faces.", "story": {"title": "Choose by the compass", "story": "On this cape the direction a room faces matters more than its stars. The west side gets the sunset and the hawkers; round the point, the east side loses the sunset and gains the quietest water on the north tip, sheltered when the kusi blows hard on the other side.", "where": "Kendwa sand and the Zalu side of Nungwi"}}, {"key": "landings", "label": "Where the boats land — and the island grows", "lead": "The auctions, the market hall and the plantation: the three places on Unguja where food is actually priced.", "story": {"title": "The auction runs on the moon", "story": "The fishery here is a monsoon-and-moon calendar, not a clock. The ngalawa and the mashua go out and come back on the tide, so the landing time walks around the day by roughly fifty minutes each morning, and the spring tides of new and full moon are the big landings. Nungwi's market is the closest; Mkokotoni, twenty kilometres south, is the district's real auction and the cheaper one. Both are finished by mid-morning. If you turn up at eleven, you are buying yesterday.", "where": "Nungwi beach at first light; Mkokotoni before 09:00"}}, {"key": "swahili", "label": "The Swahili kitchens", "lead": "Where the village eats — coconut, clove and rice at village prices, and every one of them off the sand.", "story": {"title": "Mchuzi wa nazi — the mother sauce", "story": "The base of this island's cooking is coconut: the flesh grated on a mbuzi, the 'goat' stool with a serrated blade a woman sits on and scrapes against, then pressed twice — a thick first milk and a thin second — and built into mchuzi wa nazi with garlic, ginger, turmeric, cumin and tomato. Octopus cooked in it becomes pweza wa nazi, the plate this coast is named for. The tell of a real one is that the sauce has been reduced until the oil separates and comes back to the surface; the tourist version is coconut milk poured over fish at the last minute and never split.", "where": "Combo 1990 and Machnoo in Nungwi village; Lukmaan in Stone Town"}}, {"key": "italian", "label": "The other kitchens — Italian ovens and a Hanoi broth", "lead": "Italian-run kitchens and a Vietnamese broth: the arrivals that stayed and became part of the north tip.", "story": {"title": "The other community on the coast", "story": "Italians are one of the oldest tourist communities on this coast, and the ovens on the Kendwa road and the Nungwi strip are where they eat. Judge an Italian room here on its pizza dough and its gelato, which on an island with an unsteady cold chain is harder than it looks.", "where": "Kendwa road and Nungwi beach"}}, {"key": "breakfast", "label": "The breakfast tables", "lead": "The three rooms open before nine — Kilimanjaro coffee, a French bakery, and a table on the empty end of the beach.", "story": {"title": "Two coffees, two traditions", "story": "There are two entirely different coffees on this island and they are not competing. Kahawa chungu is the Swahili one: bitter, unsweetened, spiced with cardamom and clove, kept hot in a tall brass kettle over charcoal and drunk standing from a small handleless cup that you hand straight back. Then there is Tanzanian arabica, grown on the mainland under Kilimanjaro, which arrives here as beans. One is furniture for a conversation; the other is breakfast. Have both, and do not let anyone put sugar in the first.", "where": "JF Kili's truck by the square for the arabica; the coffee men at Darajani for the kahawa chungu"}}, {"key": "grills", "label": "The fire", "lead": "Charcoal on the sand, a small hotel kitchen, and the weekly BBQ night.", "story": {"title": "The fire comes before the menu", "story": "On a coast with an unreliable cold chain, a grill is the honest kitchen: the fish is on ice in front of you, the price is agreed per kilogram before it goes on, and the charcoal does the rest. Ask for it butterflied, with lime and pilipili, and with kachumbari to cut the char.", "where": "Nungwi beach"}}, {"key": "chefs-eat", "label": "After service — where the strip eats late", "lead": "After the sunset rush: the kitchen that runs latest, lobster at a working wage, and the last room awake.", "story": {"title": "The late shift", "story": "This coast closes early. Kitchens stop between about ten and eleven, and the people who work the sunset — bar crews, dive staff, the boat boys — eat after it. The three rooms in this lane are where the last plate on the cape is still possible: order by half past ten.", "where": "Nungwi and Kendwa"}}, {"key": "beach-rooms", "label": "The sand bars", "lead": "The sundowner belt — the quiet bar on Kendwa sand and the roof in Nungwi.", "story": {"title": "Forty minutes of gold", "story": "The sundowner is a short ritual here: the sun goes into open water past Tumbatu, and for the half hour before it the bars fill and the roof seats go. Arrive early, order before the light turns, and the drink comes with the whole western channel in front of it.", "where": "Kendwa and Nungwi"}}, {"key": "story", "label": "The old houses", "lead": "The three addresses that were here before the strip was — and one that was built as a private house.", "story": {"title": "Thirty years is old here", "story": "This is a young strip on an ancient coast. Nungwi has been building boats and reading this water for centuries, but the bar-and-hotel ribbon along its western edge is barely three decades deep — which makes a bar that opened in the nineties an institution and a house that has traded thirty years the elder statesman of the beach. Hold both timescales at once: the dhow yard four hundred metres north is doing something two thousand years old, and the bar you are drinking in is younger than most of its customers.", "where": "Cholo's driftwood on the Nungwi sand; Kendwa Rocks, where the party began in 1996"}}, {"key": "street", "label": "La Rue — the street", "lead": "Cooked in front of you, eaten standing up, paid for in shillings — and priced before it is made.", "story": {"title": "The rule that makes street food safe", "story": "One rule covers everything in this group and it is not about hygiene theatre: eat only what is cooked from raw in front of you. At the Nungwi grills you point at a fish on ice and watch it go on the coals. At Forodhani a Zanzibar pizza is stretched, filled and fried while you stand there — and every stall also keeps a stack of pre-made ones under a light for people who will not wait. The difference between the best street food on the island and a bad night is the willingness to wait four minutes. Settle the price first, in shillings, before anything is lit.", "where": "The Nungwi fish-market grills from 18:00; Forodhani in Stone Town from dusk"}}];
